  faq: [
   ['Can a technician verify their own result?', 'No. The server enforces LAB_VERIFIER_MUST_DIFFER. The client disables a known matching author from the actual requisition stamp; display names alone never establish identity.'],
   ['Why is a note required when rejecting?', 'The bench technician needs clear factual instructions to resolve the defect (e.g. hemolyzed specimen, inconsistent metric values, or dilution error). The server requires a non-empty note up to 1000 characters.'],
   ['What happens when a critical result is verified?', 'Verifying a critical result triggers an immediate high-priority warning banner reminding the verifier to notify the ordering doctor immediately and record the notification in the Critical Results log.'],
   ['What is the turnaround delay reason for?', 'When a result is verified after its due time, asking for an optional delay reason (up to 500 characters) provides required clinical governance data for MSDS AAC 4 turnaround time compliance.'],
   ['How does the report reflect verification?', 'The official lab report appends the verifier verified_stamp with name, designation, and timestamp directly under the technician performed_stamp.'],
   ['Can I verify if the service is in Records mode?', 'No. Records hides the pending queue and all decision actions. Permitted known requisitions and reports remain readable through the header lookups.'],
   ['What if network drops during verification?', 'The dialog and navigation remain locked. Read current result reads a matching known requisition or the pending queue. A missing queue row does not prove publication; inspect actual result state and stamp before another action. Deliberately release after review; never replay the decision.'],
   ['Is the verification queue fully accepted?', 'Implementation and automated tests demonstrate contract compliance, but full acceptance requires live DEV journey execution, real screenshots, and named human portal review.']
  ],
